DEEP[1]=("Chapter 3 is called Karma Yoga, the yoga of action. It opens with Arjuna’s second question in the Gītā (after 2.54). He calls Kṛṣṇa both <i>Janārdana</i>, “mover of people”, and <i>Keśava</i>: two names in one verse, perhaps showing his agitation.",
 "<i>Jyāyasī cet karmaṇas te matā buddhiḥ</i>, “if you think understanding superior to action”. <i>Ghore karmaṇi</i>, “in this terrible action”. Arjuna has heard two things: “understanding is better” (2.49) and “fight” (2.18, 2.37). He wants to know which. Commentators find his question the key to the Gītā’s structure. Śaṅkara’s long introduction to chapter 3 argues that knowledge and action are for different kinds of people at different stages, so the Gītā cannot be teaching their combination for one person. Rāmānuja argues the opposite: action, done with knowledge of the self, is itself a means to liberation. Madhva sides with action done in devotion. Tilak later built his Gītā Rahasya around this chapter.",
 "Contradictions in advice are often levels in disguise. Asking “which applies to me, now?” can resolve them.")
DEEP[2]=("<i>Vyāmiśreṇeva vākyena</i>, “with words that seem mixed”. <i>Buddhiṃ mohayasīva me</i>, “you seem to confuse my understanding”. <i>Tad ekaṃ vada niścitya</i>, “tell me that one thing, decisively”.",
 "Arjuna uses <i>iva</i>, “as if”, twice. Commentators read this as courtesy toward the teacher, but also as a hint: the confusion lies in the hearer, not the teaching. <i>Śreyas</i>, the highest good, is the word Arjuna used in 2.7. Śaṅkara says Arjuna wants one path, either knowledge or action, because he cannot follow both at once. Rāmānuja says Arjuna wants to know how they fit together. The chapter will answer both: there are two paths (3.3), but action is needed by all who are not yet perfect (3.4–8), and even the perfect act for the world (3.20–25).",
 "Asking for one clear answer is fine. Be ready, though, for an answer that shows how two things fit together.")
DEEP[3]=("<i>Loke’smin dvi-vidhā niṣṭhā</i>, “in this world a twofold path”. <i>Purā proktā mayānagha</i>, “taught by me of old, sinless one”. <i>Jñāna-yogena sāṅkhyānām</i>, knowledge for the contemplatives; <i>karma-yogena yoginām</i>, action for the yogis.",
 "<i>Niṣṭhā</i> means a firm standing, a settled way of life. <i>Purā</i>, “of old”, hints that this teaching is ancient, as 4.1–3 will explain. <i>Anagha</i>, “sinless”, is a gentle encouragement: Arjuna feared sin, but Kṛṣṇa calls him sinless. Commentators debate whether the two paths are parallel or successive. Śaṅkara: successive, action first, then knowledge. Rāmānuja: both valid; karma yoga is easier and safer for most. Madhva: both culminate in devotion. Chapter 5 returns to the question (5.2–5) and concludes that the wise see the two as one. Vivekananda later spoke of four yogas, adding bhakti and rāja yoga, for four temperaments.",
 "There is more than one way up the mountain. Choose the path that suits your nature, and respect others on theirs.")
DEEP[4]=("<i>Na karmaṇām anārambhāt</i>, “not by not starting actions”. <i>Naiṣkarmyaṃ puruṣo’śnute</i>, “does a person attain actionlessness”. <i>Na ca sannyasanād eva</i>, “nor by renunciation alone”. <i>Siddhiṃ samadhigacchati</i>, “does one reach perfection”.",
 "<i>Naiṣkarmya</i>, the state beyond action, is a central Vedānta term. Sureśvara, Śaṅkara’s disciple, wrote a whole work titled Naiṣkarmya-siddhi. Commentators agree that naiṣkarmya does not mean doing nothing. It means a state where action no longer binds, because the sense of “I am the doer” is gone (4.18–20). Śaṅkara says outer renunciation without a pure mind does not bring it; inner purity comes first, through selfless action. Rāmānuja says the self is realised through action done without attachment. The verse warns against a shortcut that many seekers are tempted by: dropping duties early.",
 "Quitting does not make us free. Doing well, without grasping, does.")
DEEP[5]=("<i>Na hi kaścit kṣaṇam api</i>, “no one even for a moment”. <i>Jātu tiṣṭhaty akarma-kṛt</i>, “ever remains without acting”. <i>Kāryate hy avaśaḥ karma</i>, “is made to act helplessly”. <i>Sarvaḥ prakṛti-jair guṇaiḥ</i>, “by the guṇas born of nature”.",
 "<i>Prakṛti</i>, nature or matter, and its three <i>guṇas</i>, sattva (clarity), rajas (activity) and tamas (inertia), are the Gītā’s map of the moving world. They are explained in chapter 14. Commentators note that even sleep is a kind of action of tamas, and thinking is action of rajas or sattva. Śaṅkara adds that this applies to the unenlightened; the knower, identified with the Self, sees the guṇas acting but does not think “I act”. Rāmānuja says even the knower must act while embodied. The point for Arjuna: refusing to fight is still an action, driven by guṇas, and will have results.",
 "Since we are always acting, let us choose our actions rather than drift into them.")
DEEP[6]=("<i>Karmendriyāṇi saṃyamya</i>, “restraining the organs of action”: the five organs of action are speech, hands, feet, the organs of excretion and of generation. <i>Mithyācāraḥ</i>, “one of false conduct”, a hypocrite.",
 "<i>Manasā smaran</i>, “remembering with the mind”, dwelling on objects. Commentators link this verse to 2.59: abstaining from objects leaves the taste. The pretend renouncer sits still, but the mind keeps feeding. Śaṅkara says such a person is <i>vimūḍhātmā</i>, deluded, because he thinks outer stillness is the goal. The Gītā consistently values the inner over the outer: chapter 17 will distinguish austerity done for show (17.18) from true austerity. Many traditions have the same warning. Jesus’ words against those who pray to be seen are a familiar parallel.",
 "If you meditate or pray, notice where the mind goes. Honest wandering is better than pretend stillness.")
DEEP[7]=("<i>Yas tv indriyāṇi manasā niyamya</i>, “but one who controls the senses by the mind”. <i>Karmendriyaiḥ karma-yogam</i>, “karma yoga through the organs of action”. <i>Asaktaḥ sa viśiṣyate</i>, “unattached, he excels”.",
 "This verse defines karma yoga practically: mind in charge, senses governed, body active, heart free. Commentators contrast 3.6 and 3.7: one controls the body but not the mind; the other controls the mind and uses the body. Śaṅkara says the karma yogi is superior to the hypocrite, not to the true renunciant. Rāmānuja says the karma yogi is superior to anyone who attempts knowledge without first purifying the mind. The phrase <i>viśiṣyate</i>, “excels”, is strong praise for ordinary working life done well.",
 "Most of us will never sit in caves. The Gītā says that is fine: a well-done day of work, free of grasping, is yoga.")
DEEP[8]=("<i>Niyataṃ kuru karma tvam</i>, “do your prescribed work”. <i>Karma jyāyo hy akarmaṇaḥ</i>, “action is better than inaction”. <i>Śarīra-yātrāpi ca te na prasidhyed akarmaṇaḥ</i>, “even the journey of your body would not succeed without action”.",
 "<i>Śarīra-yātrā</i>, “the body’s journey”, is a lovely phrase for daily life. Commentators read <i>niyata karma</i> in two ways. Traditional readers take it as the duties prescribed by scripture for one’s station in life. Modern readers often take it as the work that is clearly one’s own. Śaṅkara says action is better than inaction for the unenlightened, because it purifies. Rāmānuja says action is better even for a seeker of knowledge, because knowledge without action is unsteady. The Gītā here meets common sense: even saints eat, and food takes work.",
 "Begin with the plain duties in front of you. Doing them well is already part of the path.")
DEEP[9]=("<i>Yajñārthāt karmaṇo’nyatra</i>, “except action done for yajña”. <i>Loko’yaṃ karma-bandhanaḥ</i>, “this world is bound by action”. <i>Mukta-saṅgaḥ samācara</i>, “do it, free from attachment”.",
 "This verse is the hinge of chapter 3. Commentators quote the Vedic saying <i>yajño vai viṣṇuḥ</i>, “sacrifice is Viṣṇu”. Śaṅkara and Rāmānuja both use it to read “for yajña” as “for the Lord”. Others read yajña in the broad sense of 4.24–33, where many kinds of offering are listed: of wealth, of austerity, of study, of breath. The principle is the same: action offered beyond oneself does not bind. Gandhi called his spinning a yajña. Many people today see service, <i>sevā</i>, as the living form of this verse.",
 "Choose one daily task and silently dedicate it to something larger than yourself. Watch how it changes the doing.")
DEEP[10]=("<i>Saha-yajñāḥ prajāḥ sṛṣṭvā</i>, “having created beings together with yajña”. <i>Purovāca prajāpatiḥ</i>, “Prajāpati said of old”. <i>Anena prasaviṣyadhvam</i>, “by this you will multiply”. <i>Eṣa vo’stv iṣṭa-kāmadhuk</i>, “may this be your wish-fulfilling cow”.",
 "Prajāpati, “lord of creatures”, is the creator in the Vedic texts, often identified with Brahmā. The idea that creation and sacrifice came together is old: the Puruṣa Sūkta (Ṛg Veda 10.90) describes the world itself arising from a cosmic sacrifice. Commentators read 3.10–16 as a sketch of the cosmic order of give and take. Śaṅkara treats it as an argument for the unenlightened to keep doing their duties. Rāmānuja reads Prajāpati as the Lord himself, who gives humans sacrifice as a way to reach him. Modern readers often see in it the sacredness of interdependence.",
 "Every good thing we enjoy came through someone’s effort or nature’s gift. Remembering that is the start of gratitude.")
DEEP[11]=("<i>Devān bhāvayatānena</i>, “nourish the gods with this”. <i>Te devā bhāvayantu vaḥ</i>, “may the gods nourish you”. <i>Parasparaṃ bhāvayantaḥ</i>, “nourishing one another”. <i>Śreyaḥ param avāpsyatha</i>, “you will reach the highest good”.",
 "The verb <i>bhāvay</i> means to cause to grow, to cherish, to sustain. The <i>devas</i> in the Vedic view are the shining powers in nature: Indra with the rain, Agni the fire, Sūrya the sun, Vāyu the wind. Commentators note that “the highest good” here is read by Śaṅkara as purity of mind leading to knowledge, and by Rāmānuja as the self’s release. Modern teachers have drawn ecological meaning from <i>parasparam</i>: humans and nature are partners, and damaging nature breaks the cycle that feeds us. Whatever the reading, the verse teaches mutual care, not one-way taking.",
 "Care for what cares for you: the earth, water, air, and the people around you. Mutual care is the oldest law.")
DEEP[12]=("<i>Iṣṭān bhogān hi vo devā dāsyante</i>, “the gods will give you desired enjoyments”. <i>Tair dattān apradāyaibhyaḥ</i>, “who enjoys their gifts without giving to them”. <i>Yo bhuṅkte stena eva saḥ</i>, “is a thief indeed”.",
 "The daily five great sacrifices, <i>pañca-mahā-yajña</i>, are described in the law-books (Manusmṛti 3.70). There are offerings to the gods, study of scripture for the sages, water for the ancestors, food for guests, and food for animals and birds. Commentators connect 3.12 directly to them. Śaṅkara calls the one who does not give back a thief because he takes what belongs to others. Rāmānuja says everything comes from the Lord, so enjoying without offering is theft from him. In today’s terms, many read the verse as a call to give back to community and nature, not only to consume.",
 "Count today what you received without paying: sunlight, a smile, clean water. Then find one way to give back.")
DEEP[13]=("<i>Yajña-śiṣṭāśinaḥ santaḥ</i>, “the good who eat the remnants of sacrifice”. <i>Mucyante sarva-kilbiṣaiḥ</i>, “are freed from all sins”. <i>Bhuñjate te tv aghaṃ pāpāḥ</i>, “but the sinful eat sin”. <i>Ye pacanty ātma-kāraṇāt</i>, “who cook only for themselves”.",
 "The idea is older than the Gītā. The Ṛg Veda (10.117.6) says, <i>kevalāgho bhavati kevalādī</i>: “one who eats alone is all sin”. Commentators read the verse at two levels. Ritually, food is first offered to the deity and then eaten as <i>prasāda</i>. Ethically, whatever we enjoy should first be shared. The custom of feeding a guest, a cow or a bird before eating, still kept in many homes, comes from this. Śaṅkara notes that the five daily offerings remove the small, unavoidable harms of household life, such as killing insects while cooking. The verse turns eating into worship.",
 "Before eating, pause for a moment of thanks or offering. It changes food from fuel into a gift.")
DEEP[14]=("<i>Annād bhavanti bhūtāni</i>, “from food beings arise”. <i>Parjanyād anna-sambhavaḥ</i>, “from rain, food”. <i>Yajñād bhavati parjanyaḥ</i>, “from sacrifice, rain”. <i>Yajñaḥ karma-samudbhavaḥ</i>, “sacrifice is born of action”.",
 "This chain is found in older texts too. The Manusmṛti (3.76) says an offering in fire reaches the sun, from the sun comes rain, from rain food, from food creatures. Commentators read it literally (ritual brings rain) and broadly (all life is linked). The Taittirīya Upaniṣad (3.2) calls food Brahman, and teaches reverence for food. Modern readers see in this verse an early picture of the water cycle and food chain. Whatever one thinks of ritual, the point stands: our actions feed into a great cycle on which all life depends.",
 "Next time you eat, trace the chain back: farmer, rain, sun, soil. You are part of that chain.")
DEEP[15]=("<i>Karma brahmodbhavaṃ viddhi</i>, “know action to arise from Brahman”. <i>Brahmākṣara-samudbhavam</i>, “Brahman from the Imperishable”. <i>Tasmāt sarva-gataṃ brahma</i>, “so the all-pervading Brahman”. <i>Nityaṃ yajñe pratiṣṭhitam</i>, “is ever established in sacrifice”.",
 "Commentators differ on the first “Brahman”. Śaṅkara reads it as the Veda, which teaches action. Rāmānuja reads it as the material body, prakṛti, by which actions are done, which in turn arises from the Imperishable, the self or the Lord. Madhva reads both as the Lord. All agree the chain ends in the Divine. The verse completes the circle of 3.14: beings, food, rain, sacrifice, action, Veda, Imperishable. Śaṅkara notes that <i>sarva-gata</i>, all-pervading, shows that Brahman is present in every offering. The Gītā returns to this in 4.24: the offering, the fire and the one who offers are all Brahman.",
 "The sacred is not elsewhere. It is present in honest work and sincere offering, right where we are.")
DEEP[16]=("<i>Evaṃ pravartitaṃ cakram</i>, “the wheel thus set turning”. <i>Nānuvartayatīha yaḥ</i>, “who does not follow it here”. <i>Aghāyur indriyārāmaḥ</i>, “sinful in life, delighting in the senses”. <i>Moghaṃ pārtha sa jīvati</i>, “lives in vain, Pārtha”.",
 "The image of the wheel, <i>cakra</i>, is powerful: life as a cycle of giving and receiving in which every being has a part. Commentators note that the person who breaks the wheel is described as <i>indriyārāma</i>, one whose garden of delight is the senses. Śaṅkara says such a person, not yet fit for renunciation, must do his part; refusing is sin. Rāmānuja says such a one wastes the rare human birth. The Buddhist image of the Dharma wheel and the Gītā’s wheel of yajña are different ideas, but both picture an order that one should join, not resist.",
 "Ask today: am I turning the wheel or only riding it? Small contributions count.")
DEEP[17]=("<i>Yas tv ātma-ratir eva syāt</i>, “but one whose delight is in the Self alone”. <i>Ātma-tṛptaś ca</i>, “satisfied in the Self”. <i>Ātmany eva ca santuṣṭaḥ</i>, “content in the Self alone”. <i>Tasya kāryaṃ na vidyate</i>, “for him there is nothing to be done”.",
 "Three words describe the realised: <i>rati</i>, delight; <i>tṛpti</i>, satisfaction; <i>santoṣa</i>, contentment, all in the Self. Commentators note this matches 2.55. Śaṅkara builds his reading here: the knower has no obligatory duties, and may renounce. Rāmānuja says the verse describes a goal, not a present state for Arjuna, who must still act. Madhva notes that even the free soul serves God out of love. The Muṇḍaka Upaniṣad (3.1.4) describes the knower of Brahman as <i>ātma-krīḍa ātma-ratiḥ kriyāvān</i>: “playing in the Self, delighting in the Self, yet active”.",
 "The deepest contentment does not stop us acting. It changes why we act: from need to joy.")
DEEP[18]=("<i>Naiva tasya kṛtenārthaḥ</i>, “he has no purpose in action”. <i>Nākṛteneha kaścana</i>, “nor in non-action here”. <i>Na cāsya sarva-bhūteṣu kaścid artha-vyapāśrayaḥ</i>, “nor does he depend on any being for anything”.",
 "This continues 3.17. Commentators note the balance: the free person gains nothing by acting and loses nothing by not acting. That very freedom makes his action pure. Śaṅkara says that for such a person, duty imposed from outside falls away. Rāmānuja adds that such a person still acts for the world’s sake, as the next verses show. The verse also answers a practical worry: if I stop needing approval, will I stop working? The Gītā says no. One works more freely, because one is no longer working to fill a lack.",
 "Notice how much of your effort is for approval. Imagine doing the same work without needing it. What changes?")
DEEP[19]=("<i>Tasmād asaktaḥ satatam</i>, “therefore, always unattached”. <i>Kāryaṃ karma samācara</i>, “do the work that must be done”. <i>Asakto hy ācaran karma</i>, “acting without attachment”. <i>Param āpnoti pūruṣaḥ</i>, “a person reaches the Highest”.",
 "<i>Tasmāt</i>, “therefore”, links back to 3.17–18. Even though the free person needs nothing, you, Arjuna, are still on the way, so act without attachment. Commentators highlight <i>kāryam</i>, “what must be done”, the duty at hand, not just any action. Śaṅkara says unattached action leads to the Highest by purifying the mind for knowledge. Rāmānuja says it leads directly to the vision of the self. Many readers find this verse the practical heart of chapter 3. The method is simple to state and hard to live: do what is yours, fully, without clinging.",
 "Write down the one thing that must be done today. Do it with full care and no worry about praise.")
DEEP[20]=("Janaka, king of Videha (Mithilā), is famous in the Upaniṣads as a royal sage. In the Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad he learns from the sage Yājñavalkya and gives him a thousand cows for each teaching. He is also remembered as the father of Sītā in the Rāmāyaṇa.",
 "<i>Karmaṇaiva hi saṃsiddhim āsthitā janakādayaḥ</i>, “by action alone Janaka and others reached perfection”. <i>Loka-saṅgraham</i>, “holding the world together”. Commentators read <i>loka-saṅgraha</i> as one of the Gītā’s great social ideas. Even a free person acts so that society stays in order and others are guided. Śaṅkara says Janaka and others were already knowers who continued to act for the world. Rāmānuja says they reached perfection through action. A famous story tells that when Mithilā burned, Janaka said, “nothing of mine burns”. Tilak and Gandhi built their ideal of engaged spirituality on this verse.",
 "Ask whom your work holds up. Even if you need nothing for yourself, others may need your example.")
DEEP[21]=("<i>Yad yad ācarati śreṣṭhaḥ</i>, “whatever the best person does”. <i>Tat tad evetaro janaḥ</i>, “that and that only other people do”. <i>Sa yat pramāṇaṃ kurute</i>, “whatever standard he sets”. <i>Lokas tad anuvartate</i>, “the world follows it”.",
 "This verse is often quoted on leadership and parenting. Commentators note the double meaning of <i>śreṣṭha</i>: the best, and the leading. Śaṅkara applies it to the knower whose conduct guides others. Rāmānuja applies it to anyone respected for wisdom. For Arjuna the point is sharp: he is the greatest warrior of his age; if he drops his bow, others will follow, and the Pāṇḍava cause collapses. A Sanskrit saying has the same idea: <i>yathā rājā tathā prajā</i>, “as the king, so the people”.",
 "Someone is watching how you work, speak and handle stress. Let what they learn be worth copying.")
DEEP[22]=("<i>Na me pārthāsti kartavyam</i>, “I have no duty, Pārtha”. <i>Triṣu lokeṣu kiñcana</i>, “in the three worlds, nothing”. <i>Nānavāptam avāptavyam</i>, “nothing ungained to be gained”. <i>Varta eva ca karmaṇi</i>, “yet I engage in action”.",
 "Here Kṛṣṇa uses himself as the example for the first time. Commentators love this verse. The Lord, full and complete, keeps acting: creating, sustaining, guiding. In his life as Kṛṣṇa he drove chariots, washed guests’ feet at Yudhiṣṭhira’s sacrifice, carried messages of peace and tended cows. Rāmānuja sees here the Lord’s accessibility, <i>saulabhya</i>: he takes on ordinary tasks out of love. Śaṅkara notes that the Lord acts for the welfare of the world. The verse prepares for chapter 4, where Kṛṣṇa reveals his divine births and works (4.9).",
 "If God himself works without needing to, then work done with love needs no other justification.")
DEEP[23]=("<i>Yadi hy ahaṃ na varteyam</i>, “if I did not engage”. <i>Jātu karmaṇy atandritaḥ</i>, “ever, tirelessly, in action”. <i>Mama vartmānuvartante</i>, “people would follow my path”. <i>Manuṣyāḥ pārtha sarvaśaḥ</i>, “humans in every way, Pārtha”.",
 "<i>Mama vartmānuvartante manuṣyāḥ pārtha sarvaśaḥ</i> is a half-line that recurs at 4.11. There it means “in every way people follow my path”, a statement of all paths leading to God. Here the sense is more practical: everyone copies the example set at the top. Commentators connect the two uses: the Lord is both the model of action and the goal of every path. <i>Atandrita</i>, “without sloth”, is a word of untiring energy. Śaṅkara stresses that the Lord does not need to act, but chooses to for the world. Madhva notes the Lord’s action is always free, never forced.",
 "Steady, quiet work done day after day is itself an example. People notice consistency more than speeches.")
DEEP[24]=("<i>Utsīdeyur ime lokāḥ</i>, “these worlds would fall into ruin”. <i>Na kuryāṃ karma ced aham</i>, “if I did not act”. <i>Saṅkarasya ca kartā syām</i>, “I would be the cause of confusion”. <i>Upahanyām imāḥ prajāḥ</i>, “I would destroy these beings”.",
 "<i>Saṅkara</i>, confusion or mixing, recalls Arjuna’s fear of <i>varṇa-saṅkara</i> in 1.41. Commentators note the reversal: Arjuna feared that fighting would cause confusion; Kṛṣṇa says the real cause of confusion is the abandonment of duty by those who should lead. Śaṅkara reads the verse as the Lord’s concern for the world’s order. Rāmānuja reads it as an argument for Arjuna: if even the Lord must act lest the world fail, how much more must a warrior? The verse quietly answers chapter 1’s fear of chaos.",
 "When the responsible step back, disorder follows. Showing up is a quiet act of care for everyone else.")
DEEP[25]=("<i>Saktāḥ karmaṇy avidvāṃsaḥ</i>, “as the unwise, attached to action”. <i>Yathā kurvanti bhārata</i>, “act, Bhārata”. <i>Kuryād vidvāṃs tathāsaktaś</i>, “so should the wise act, unattached”. <i>Cikīrṣur loka-saṅgraham</i>, “wishing to hold the world together”.",
 "Commentators find a fine balance here. The wise should work with the same energy as the unwise, not less. But the motive is different: not personal gain, but <i>loka-saṅgraha</i>, the welfare of the world. Śaṅkara says the wise act this way to avoid misleading others. Rāmānuja adds that the wise do it to inspire others to start karma yoga. Gandhi and Vinoba Bhave applied this to public life: work hard, own nothing, serve all. The verse also corrects a common misreading of detachment as half-heartedness.",
 "Detachment does not mean doing less. Give full effort; just don’t tie your peace to the result.")
